WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:05.340
So thanks a lot Peter for joining me today.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:12.340
I think a lot of people know you as a well-known machine learning and deep learning and robotics researcher.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:17.340
I'd like to have people hear a bit about your story. How did you end up doing the work that you do?

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:24.340
It's a good question and actually if you would have asked me as a 14 year old what I was aspiring to do,

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:31.340
it probably would not have been this. In fact, at the time I thought being a professional basketball player would be the right way to go.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.340
I don't think I was able to achieve it.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:38.340
I feel the machine learning left out that the basketball team didn't work out.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:44.340
Yeah, that didn't work out. It was a lot of fun playing basketball, but it didn't work out to try to make it into a career.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.340
So what I really liked in school was physics and math.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:55.340
And so from there it seemed pretty natural to study engineering, which is applying physics and math in the real world.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:01:04.340
And actually then after my undergrad in electrical engineering, I wasn't so sure what to do because literally anything engineering seemed interesting to me.

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:09.340
Like understanding how anything works seems interesting. Trying to build anything is interesting.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:18.340
And in some sense artificial intelligence won out because it seemed like it could somehow help all disciplines in some way.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:22.340
And also it seemed somehow a little more at the core of everything.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:30.340
Like if you think about how a machine can think, then maybe that's more the core of everything else than picking any specific discipline.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:37.340
I've been saying AI is the new electricity. It sounds like the 14 year old version of you had an earlier version of that even.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:43.340
In the past few years you've done a lot of work in deep reinforcement learning.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:48.340
What's happening? Why is deep reinforcement learning suddenly taking off?

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:52.340
Before I worked in deep reinforcement learning, I worked a lot in reinforcement learning.

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:56.340
Actually with you Andrew at Stanford of course.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.340
And so we worked on autonomous helicopter flight.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:05.340
Then later at Berkeley with some of my students we worked on getting a robot to learn to fold laundry.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:12.340
And kind of what characterized the work was a combination of learning that enabled things that would not be possible without learning.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:17.340
But also a lot of domain expertise in combination with the learning to get this to work.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:23.340
And it was very interesting because you needed domain expertise which is fun to acquire.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.340
But at the same time it was very time consuming.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:31.340
For every new application you wanted to succeed at you needed domain expertise plus machine learning expertise.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:40.340
And for me it was in 2012 with the ImageNet breakthrough results from Jeff Hinton's group in Toronto, AlexNet,

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:48.340
showing that supervised learning all of a sudden could be done with far less engineering for the domain at hand.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:51.340
There was very little engineering about vision in AlexNet.

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:56.340
It made me think we really should revisit reinforcement learning under the same kind of viewpoint

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:05.340
and see if we can get the deep version of reinforcement learning to work and do equally interesting things as had just happened in deep supervised learning.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:11.340
So it sounds like you saw earlier than most people the potential of deep reinforcement learning.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:15.340
So now looking into the future, what do you see next?

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:20.340
What are your predictions for the next several ways to come in deep reinforcement learning?

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:26.340
So I think what's interesting about deep reinforcement learning is that in some sense there are many more questions than in supervised learning.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:29.340
In supervised learning it's about learning an input-output mapping.

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:34.340
In reinforcement learning there is the notion of where does the data even come from.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.340
So that's the exploration problem.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:38.340
When you have data, how do you do credit assignment?

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.340
How do you understand what actions you took early on?

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:42.340
Got you the reward later.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:44.340
And then there's issues of safety.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:49.340
When you have a system autonomously collecting data, essentially rather dangerous in most situations.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:53.340
Imagine a self-driving car company that says we're just going to run deep reinforcement learning.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:57.340
It's pretty likely that car would get into a lot of accidents before it does anything useful.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:00.340
You need the negative examples that are from race.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:02.340
You do need some negative examples somehow, yeah.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.340
And positive ones hopefully.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:12.340
So I think there is still a lot of challenges in deep reinforcement learning in terms of working out some of the specifics of how to get these things to work.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.340
So the deep part is the representation.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:18.340
But then the reinforcement learning itself still has a lot of questions.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:26.340
And what I feel is that with the advances in deep learning, somehow one part of the puzzle in reinforcement learning has been largely addressed.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:29.340
Which is the representation part.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:34.340
So if there is a pattern, we can probably represent it with a deep network and capture that pattern.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:39.340
And then how to tease apart the pattern is still a big challenge in reinforcement learning.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:45.340
So I think big challenges are how to get systems to reason over long time horizons.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:50.340
So right now, a lot of the successes in deep reinforcement learning are very short horizon.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:57.340
They are problems where if you act well over a five second horizon, you act well over the entire problem.

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:06.340
And so a five second skill is something very different from a day long skill or the ability to live a life as a robot or some software agent.

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:08.340
So I think there is a lot of challenges there.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:17.340
I think safety has a lot of challenges in terms of how do you learn safely and also how do you keep learning once you are already pretty good.

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:21.340
So give an example again that a lot of people would be familiar with.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:22.340
Cell driving cars.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:31.340
For a cell driving car to be better than a human driver, actually human drivers may be getting into accidents, bad accidents every three million miles or something.

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:37.340
And so that takes a long time to see the negative data once you are as good as a human driver.

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:40.340
But you want your cell driving car to be better than a human driver.

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:47.340
And so at that point, the data collection becomes really, really difficult to get that interesting data that makes your system improve.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:51.340
So there's a lot of challenges related to exploration that tie into that.

00:05:51.340 --> 00:06:02.340
But one of the things I'm actually most excited about right now is seeing if we can actually take a step back and also learn the reinforcement learning algorithm.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:04.340
So reinforcement learning is very complex.

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:05.340
Credit design is very complex.

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:07.340
Exploration is very complex.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:22.340
And so maybe just like how deep learning for supervised learning was able to replace a lot of domain expertise, maybe we can have programs that are learned, that are reinforcement learning programs and that do all this instead of us designing the details.

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:25.340
Do you learn the reward function or learn the whole program?

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:28.340
So this would be learning the entire reinforcement learning program.

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:36.340
So it would be, imagine you have a reinforcement learning program, whatever it is, and you throw it at some problem.

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:38.340
And then you see how long it takes to learn.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:40.340
And then you say, well, that took a while.

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:48.340
Now let another program modify this reinforcement learning program after the modification, see how fast it learns.

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:54.340
If it learns more quickly, that was a good modification and maybe keep it and improve from there.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:55.340
Wow. I see. Right. Yeah.

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:56.340
In a vicious direction.

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:57.340
Yeah.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:01.340
I think it has a lot to do with maybe the amount of compute that's becoming available.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:05.340
So this would be running reinforcement learning in the inner loop.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:08.340
Whereas right now we run reinforcement learning as the final thing.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:17.340
And so the more compute we get, the more it becomes possible to maybe run something like reinforcement learning in the inner loop of a bigger algorithm.

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:19.340
That makes sense.

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:25.340
So starting from the 14 euro U, you've worked in AI for maybe what, some 20 plus years now.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:32.340
So tell me a bit about how your understanding of AI has evolved over this time.

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:41.340
Yeah. So when I started looking at AI, it's very interesting because it really coincided with coming to Stanford to do my master's degree there.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:52.340
And there were some icons there like John McCarthy, who I got to talk with, but who had a very different approach in the year 2000 for what most people were doing at the time.

00:07:52.340 --> 00:08:04.340
But also talking with Daphne Koller. And I think a lot of my initial thinking of AI was shaped by Daphne's thinking, her AI class, her probabilistic graphical models class,

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:19.340
and kind of really being intrigued by how simply a distribution over many random variables and then being able to condition on some sets of variables and throw on conclusions about others could actually give you so much if you can somehow make it computationally tractable,

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:23.340
which was definitely the challenge to make it computable.

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:28.340
And then from there, when I started my PhD, Andrew, you arrived at Stanford.

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:41.340
And I think you gave me a really good reality check. That's not the right metric to evaluate your work by and to really try to see the connection from what you're working on to what impact it can really have,

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:46.340
what change it can make rather than what's the math that happened to be in your work.

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:50.340
Right. That's amazing. I did not realize I had forgotten that.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:59.340
Yeah, it's actually one of the things that I cite most often. People ask, what's if you're going to cite only one thing that has stuck with you from Andrew's advice.

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:05.340
It's making sure you can see the connection to where it's actually going to do something.

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:10.340
You know, you've had and you're continuing to have an amazing career in AI.

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:21.340
So for some of the people listening to you on video now, if they want to also enter or pursue a career in AI, what advice do you have for them?

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:24.340
I think it's a really good time to get into artificial intelligence.

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:34.340
If you look at the demand for people, it's so high. There are so many job opportunities, so many things you can do research-wise, build new companies and so forth.

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:39.340
So I would say, yes, it's definitely a smart decision in terms of actually getting going.

00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:45.340
A lot of it you can self-study, whether you're in school or not. There is a lot of online courses. There is your machine learning course.

00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:55.340
There is also, for example, Andrew Carapati's deep learning course, which has videos online, which is a great way to get started.

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:59.340
Berkeley does the deep reinforcement learning course, which is all the lectures online.

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:01.340
So those are all good places to get started.

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:06.340
I think a big part of what's important is to make sure you try things yourself.

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:10.340
So not just read things or watch videos, but try things out.

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:22.340
With frameworks like TensorFlow, Chainer, Fiano, PyTorch and so forth, whatever is your favorite, it's very easy to get going and get something up and running very quickly.

00:10:22.340 --> 00:10:27.340
To get to practice yourself, implementing and seeing what works and seeing what doesn't work.

00:10:27.340 --> 00:10:35.340
So this past week there was an article in Mashable about a 16-year-old in the United Kingdom who is one of the leaders on Kaggle competitions.

00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:44.340
He just said, he just went out and found things online, learned everything himself and never actually took any formal course, per se.

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:50.340
And there he is as a 16-year-old just being very competitive in Kaggle competitions. So it's definitely possible.

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:54.340
Yeah. We live in good times for people that want to learn.

00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:55.340
Absolutely.

00:10:55.340 --> 00:11:01.340
One question I bet you get asked sometimes is, if someone wants to enter AI machine learning deep learning,

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:06.340
should they apply for a PhD program or should they get a job at a big company?

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:12.340
I think a lot of it has to do with maybe how much mentoring you can get.

00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:20.340
So in a PhD program, you're essentially guaranteed the job of the professor is, who is your advisor, is to look out for you,

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:28.340
try to do everything they can to kind of shape you, help you, become stronger at whatever you want to do, for example, AI.

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:33.340
And so there's a very clear, dedicated person. Sometimes you have two advisors, and that's literally a job,

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:40.340
and that's why they are professors. That's most of what they like about being professors often is helping shape students to become more capable at things.

00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:45.340
Now, it doesn't mean it's not possible at companies, and many companies have really good mentors

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:51.340
and have people who love to help educate people who come in and strengthen them and so forth.

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:59.340
It might not be as much of a guarantee and a given compared to actually enrolling in a PhD program where that's,

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:05.340
the crux of the program is that you're going to learn and somebody is there to help you learn.

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:09.340
Yeah, so it really depends on the company and depends on the PhD program.

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:15.340
Absolutely, yeah. But I think it is key that you can learn a lot on your own, but I think you can learn a lot faster

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:24.340
if you have somebody who's more experienced, who's actually taking it up as their responsibility to spend time with you and help accelerate your progress.

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:28.340
So you've been one of the most visible leaders in deep reinforcement learning.

00:12:28.340 --> 00:12:33.340
So what are the things that deep reinforcement learning is already working really well at?

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:38.340
I think if you look at some deep reinforcement learning successes, it's very, very intriguing.

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:48.340
For example, learning to play Atari games from pixels, processing these pixels, which is just numbers that are being processed somehow and turn into joystick actions.

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:55.340
Then, for example, some of the work we did at Berkeley where we have a simulated robot inventing walking,

00:12:55.340 --> 00:13:01.340
and the reward that it's given is as simple as the further you go north, the better, and the less hard you impact with the ground, the better.

00:13:01.340 --> 00:13:05.340
And somehow it decides that walking slash running is the thing to invent,

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:09.340
whereas nobody showed it what walking is or running is.

00:13:09.340 --> 00:13:16.340
Or a robot playing with children's toys and learning to kind of put them together, put a block into a matching opening and so forth.

00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:26.340
And so I think it's really interesting that in all of these, it's possible to learn from raw sensory inputs all the way to raw controls, for example, torques at the motors.

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:32.340
But at the same time, so it's very interesting that you can have a single algorithm.

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:42.340
For example, trust region policy optimization can have a robot learn to run, can have a robot learn to stand up, can have, instead of a two-legged robot now, you're swapping a four-legged robot.

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:46.340
You run the same reinforcement algorithm, and it still learns to run.

00:13:46.340 --> 00:13:51.340
And so there's no change in the reinforcement algorithm. It's very, very general. Same for the Atari games.

00:13:51.340 --> 00:13:54.340
DQN was the same DQN for every one of the games.

00:13:54.340 --> 00:14:01.340
But then when it actually starts hitting the frontiers of what's not yet possible is, well, it's nice.

00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:03.340
It learns from scratch for each one of these tasks.

00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:09.340
But it would be even nicer if it could reuse things it's learned in the past to learn even more quickly for the next task.

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:15.340
And that's something that's still at the frontier and not yet possible. It always starts from scratch, essentially.

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:25.340
How quickly do you think you see deep reinforcement learning get deployed in the robots around us, or the robots, and they're getting deployed in the world today?

00:14:25.340 --> 00:14:33.340
I think in practice, the realistic scenario is one where it starts with supervised learning, behavioral cloning.

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:41.340
Humans do the work. And I think actually a lot of businesses will be built that way, where it's a human behind the scenes doing a lot of the work.

00:14:42.340 --> 00:14:49.340
Imagine Facebook Messenger Assistant, a system like that could be built with a human behind the curtains doing a lot of the work.

00:14:49.340 --> 00:14:56.340
Machine learning matches up with what the human does and starts making suggestions to the human so the human has a small number of options.

00:14:56.340 --> 00:15:04.340
The development can just click and select. And then over time, as it gets pretty good, you start infusing some reinforcement learning where you give it actual objectives,

00:15:04.340 --> 00:15:08.340
not just matching the human behind the curtains, but give it objectives of achievement.

00:15:09.340 --> 00:15:17.340
Maybe how fast were these two people able to plan their meeting, or how fast were they able to book their flight, or things like that?

00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:19.340
How long did it take? How happy were they with it?

00:15:19.340 --> 00:15:27.340
But it would probably have to be a lot of behavioral cloning of humans showing how this could be done.

00:15:27.340 --> 00:15:32.340
So start with behavioral cloning, just supervised learning to mimic whatever the person is doing,

00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:37.340
and then gradually layer on the reinforcement learning to have it think about longer time horizons.

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:43.340
I'd say so. Just because straight up reinforcement learning from scratch is really fun to watch.

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:50.340
It's super intriguing and very few things more fun to watch than a reinforcement learning robot starting from nothing and inventing things.

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:53.340
But it's just time consuming and it's not always safe.

00:15:53.340 --> 00:15:57.340
Thank you very much. That was fascinating. I'm really glad we had the chance to chat.

00:15:57.340 --> 00:16:00.340
Well, Andrew, thank you for having me. Very much appreciate it.
